6569: \begin{proposition}[Primary-inventory freshness criterion]
6570: \label{prop:primary-inventory-freshness-criterion}
6571: Let $S$ be a later edited version of \path{submission_abstract.tex}, and let
6572: $I$ be a primary-claim inventory with rows of the same form as
6573: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory.json}: each row gives a location in
6574: $S$, a claim text or bounded paraphrase, a row status, a certificate surface, and
6575: an evidence level.  Suppose a manual claim audit of $S$ produces a finite list of
6576: load-bearing assertions and verifies the following three conditions:
6577: \begin{enumerate}
6578: \item every load-bearing assertion in $S$ appears in exactly one row of $I$;
6579: \item each row of $I$ points to a supplement theorem, table row, manifest row, or
6580: bounded-context status that exists in the submitted package; and
6581: \item for every row, the status and evidence level do not exceed what its cited
6582: certificate surface proves under the finite record semantics of
6583: the schema-relative finite-record exactness theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}.
6584: \end{enumerate}
6585: Then the row-classification conclusion of
6586: Proposition~\ref{prop:primary-claim-row-classification} holds for the edited
6587: primary artifact $S$ with $I$ in place of the current inventory.  Conversely, if
6588: one of the three audit conditions fails, the edited primary artifact has a finite
6589: review obstruction: an unclassified load-bearing assertion, a stale certificate
6590: pointer, or an evidence-level overclaim.
6591: 
6592: For the present source state after the Stage--A theoremization round--2 edits on
6593: 2026-06-07, the current inventory is additionally witnessed at command-run freshness level by
6594: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory_freshness_2026-06-09.log}.  The
6595: recorded command is
6596: \path{python review_bundle/verify_primary_claim_inventory.py}; the recorded
6597: environment is Python 3.10.11 on Windows 10 in this paper directory; the recorded
6598: exit code is 0; and the log records 161 primary-source lines, 12 inventory rows,
6599: and 0 freshness errors.  The regenerated inventory rows are locked to the current
6600: primary-source spans \path{pc1}:30--47, \path{pc2}:53--64,
6601: \path{pc3}:66--69, \path{pc4}:68--69, \path{pc5}:70--72,
6602: \path{pc6}:72--74, \path{pc7}:82--96, \path{pc8}:98--106,
6603: \path{pc9}:108--117, \path{pc10}:119--132, \path{pc11}:134--154, and
6604: \path{pc12}:143--154.  This command-run claim is limited to row-span and
6605: certificate-field freshness for the primary inventory; it is not a full semantic
6606: proof of every primary sentence.
6607: \end{proposition}
6608: 
6609: \begin{proof}
6610: Assume the three audit conditions.  By condition~1, the audit list is a finite
6611: partition of the load-bearing assertions of $S$ by inventory row: no assertion is
6612: omitted and no assertion is assigned to two incompatible rows.  By condition~2,
6613: each row has a concrete certificate surface inside the submitted package, so the
6614: row is not supported by prose memory or by an absent local file.  By condition~3,
6615: the certificate surface licenses exactly the row's declared status and evidence
6616: level in the sense of the schema-relative finite-record exactness theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}.  Hence each
6617: load-bearing assertion of $S$ is either an accepted certificate row, a bounded
6618: contextual interface row, or an accepted bounded artifact row, according to the
6619: status permitted by its cited certificate.  Since the finite audit list exhausts
6620: all load-bearing assertions, there is no hidden primary assertion left to
6621: classify.
6622: 
6623: For the converse, if condition~1 fails, some load-bearing assertion is missing
6624: from the inventory or is assigned inconsistently, giving an unclassified-row
6625: obstruction.  If condition~2 fails, a row points outside the submitted theorem,
6626: table, manifest, or bounded-context surfaces, giving a stale-pointer obstruction.
6627: If condition~3 fails, the row asserts a stronger status or evidence level than
6628: its certificate permits; the schema-relative finite-record exactness theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, supplies a
6629: finite bad subrecord, and Theorem~\ref{thm:evidence-level-overclaim-obstruction}
6630: classifies it as an evidence-level overclaim when the excess is a stronger
6631: verification or command-run reading.  These are finite obstructions read from the
6632: edited source, the inventory row, and the cited certificate surface.
6633: 
6634: For the current command-run instantiation, the cited freshness log is itself a
6635: finite submitted record: it names the command class, environment, primary source,
6636: inventory source, line count, row count, error count, and exit code.  An exit code
6637: 0 with error count 0 is exactly the success condition of the comparison command,
6638: so the command-run freshness statement is licensed at the limited evidence level
6639: declared in the proposition.\end{proof}
