7172: \begin{theorem}[Workspace-to-final-package reconciliation]
7173: \label{thm:workspace-final-package-reconciliation}
7174: Let $D_0$ be the digest manifest
7175: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}, and let $H_i$ denote the source
7176: state printed in verifier log $i$.  The source-state claim made in Audit
7177: record~\ref{prop:final-immutable-package-state-certificate} is the finite package
7178: claim consisting of these recorded pairs $(H_i,D_0)$, not a claim that every
7179: later reviewer checkout, editor workspace, or post-packaging working tree has Git
7180: \path{HEAD} equal to a single historical commit.  If a current workspace reports
7181: a different Git \path{HEAD} from one of the historical rows, then the verifier-log and
7182: bundle-backed claims of this supplement remain admissible exactly in the
7183: following two reconciled cases:
7184: \begin{enumerate}
7185: \item the submitted support surface is exact inclusion of \path{review_bundle/}, or
7186: an archive equivalent to it, whose finite certificate files match the SHA-256
7187: rows of $D_0$; or
7188: \item the package is deliberately advanced to the current workspace state by
7189: rerunning the three verifier commands and replacing the displayed source-state
7190: record, the verifier logs, and the digest manifest by new matching
7191: source-state--manifest certificates.
7192: \end{enumerate}
7193: In all other cases a mismatch between a later workspace \path{HEAD} and a
7194: recorded historical row does not certify a new package state and does not
7195: invalidate the historical finite package claim $(H_i,D_0)$; it only shows that
7196: the later workspace is
7197: outside the submitted support surface.
7198: \end{theorem}
